[R0085] year=2025 | venue=Conference of Open Innovation Association, FRUCT | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Open-Source Large Language Model Frameworks for Automated Penetration Testing: Opportunities, Challenges, and Solutions
KEYWORDS: Artificial intelligence; Artificial intelligence technologies; Controlled simulation; Language model; Large-scales; Model-based OPC; Modelling framework; Open source frameworks; Open-source; Penetration testing; Security assessment; Security of data
ABSTRACT: The rapid development of artificial intelligence technologies, particularly Large Language Models, has a growing impact on the field of information security. One of the promising directions of their application is the automation of penetration testing - a controlled simulation of intruder actions to identify vulnerabilities in protected information systems.This study investigates the practical applicability of existing open-source frameworks based on Large Language Models, both large-scale and lightweight models, within the context of penetration testing tasks that are critical to commercial security assessments.The results demonstrated the potential application and constraints of these frameworks, addressing fundamental challenges in penetration testing and outline possible solutions for overcoming the identified limitations. Based on our results, we propose a practical approach to address key limitations and showcase the potential of Large Language Models based frameworks in real-world penetration testing. © 2025 FRUCT Oy.

[R0086] year=2026 | venue=Information (Switzerland) | type=Article
TITLE: Agentic AI Security in Industry 5.0: Emerging Threats, Forensic Readiness, and Trustworthy Human–Agent Collaboration
KEYWORDS: agentic AI; cybersecurity; digital forensics; forensic readiness; human-centric manufacturing; human–agent collaboration; industry 5.0; threat taxonomy; Artificial intelligence; Autonomous agents; Computer crime; Cybersecurity; Digital forensics; Electronic crime countermeasures; Forensic engineering; Industrial research; Intelligent agents; Taxonomies; Agent collaboration; Agentic AI; Cyber security; Forensic readiness; Human agent; Human-centric; Human-centric manufacturing; Human–agent collaboration; Industry 5.0; Threat taxonomy; Economic and social effects
ABSTRACT: Industry 5.0 places autonomous agents inside its human–agent collaborative loop, resting its human-centricity pillar on an assumption of trustworthy collaboration that has not been examined critically. Agentic artificial intelligence has moved from research demonstration to industrial deployment within months, introducing a threat class this setting has not yet addressed. This article bridges three literatures developed in isolation, Industry 5.0 cybersecurity, agentic AI security, and Industry 5.0’s own foundational scholarship, proposing a six-category threat taxonomy and a paired forensic readiness framework, formalised as a five-level maturity model and grounded in real 2024–2026 incidents rather than hypothetical scenarios. Both contributions are evaluated through two complementary methods. An exploratory elicitation exercise, modelled on Delphi methodology and using six independent large language models as blind panellists across two rounds, converges on a specific structural critique that is incorporated into the taxonomy’s final design. A retrospective coding exercise then applies that taxonomy to fourteen publicly documented incidents, finding that half require multi-category classification and that two categories remain unexercised in the current public record, evidence that physically embodied industrial deployment has outpaced the documented incident base rather than a gap in the taxonomy itself. This urgency is reinforced by emerging EU and UK regulation imposing 24- and 72-h incident reporting obligations that, on current evidence, most industrial organisations are not positioned to meet. The article closes with a research agenda addressing liability, evidentiary standards, and the readiness-sustainability trade-off. © 2026 by the authors.

[R0087] year=2026 | venue=7th International Conference on Inventive Research in Computing Applications, ICIRCA 2026 - Proceedings | type=Conference paper
TITLE: An Adaptive Edge-AI-Enabled Ionizing Radiation Sensing Architecture for Distributed Nanosatellite and Autonomous Micro-Platform Systems
KEYWORDS: Autonomous Programming; Digital Twin; Edge Intelligence; Hierarchical RL; Human-Robot Collaboration; Industrial Robotics; Industry 5.0; Large Language Models; Reinforcement Learning; Safe RL; 5G mobile communication systems; Anomaly detection; Behavioral research; Data privacy; Deep learning; Digital twin; Edge computing; Fault tolerant computer systems; Federated learning; Green computing; Industrial robots; Ionizing radiation; Man machine systems; Nanosatellites; Network security; Resource allocation; Robot programming; Scalability; Autonomous programming; Edge intelligence; Hierarchical RL; Human-robot collaboration; Industrial robotics; Industry 5.0; Language model; Large language model; Reinforcement learnings; Safe RL; Reinforcement learning
ABSTRACT: The widespread deployment of nanosatellites, micro-drones, autonomous vehicles and beyond-5G/6G wireless networks requires efficient, scalable, and energy-aware monitoring and resource allocation. Conventional systems based on a centralised architecture have high latency, communication overhead, low scalability and low adaptability. To overcome these limitations, this study introduces an Edge-AI-powered Federated Deep Reinforcement Learning (FDRL) approach for real-time monitoring of radiation and energy-efficient wireless resource allocation. The proposed framework adopts a decentralized approach by combining distributed ionizing radiation monitoring sensors, edge computing platforms, federated learning, lightweight deep neural networks, and reinforcement learning for local intelligence and autonomous learning. A layered edge computing approach enables local data processing with global context awareness through federated learning. The spectrum and environment monitoring problems are redefined using Markov Decision Process (MDP)-based optimization to provide a tradeoff between detection accuracy, spectral efficiency, energy efficiency and Quality of Service (QoS). The framework reduces communication overhead, ensures privacy, and improves scalability in dynamic environments. Experimental and simulation results show substantial performance gains with spectral efficiency of 9.1 bps/Hz, energy efficiency of 91.3%, throughput of 195 Mbps, and QoS satisfaction of 96.8% while latency is reduced from 15 ms to 8 ms and communication overhead from 18% to 8%. Scalability tests also verify the stable convergence and improved training accuracy with the increasing number of distributed edge nodes. The use of federated intelligence and edge AI allows for efficient real-time anomaly detection, resource allocation, and autonomous fault-tolerant operation on diverse platforms. In summary, the proposed framework offers a scalable, privacy-preserving and intelligent approach for future space systems, critical environment monitoring, and beyond 5G/6G wireless communication networks. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0088] year=2025 | venue=IEEE Global Engineering Education Conference, EDUCON | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Integrating Generative AI in Cybersecurity Curricula
KEYWORDS: Cybersecurity; Diffusion Models; Generative AI; LLMs; Penetration Testing; Artificial intelligence; Cognitive systems; Computer architecture; Cybersecurity; Digital forensics; Engineering education; Malware; Network security; Teaching; Artificial intelligence technologies; Cyber security; Diffusion model; Generative AI; LLM; Penetration testing; Security Architecture; Security frameworks; Security threats; Threat assessment; Curricula
ABSTRACT: Artificial intelligence technologies with generative capabilities have accelerated fundamental transformations in security architectures, necessitating reconceptualization of security frameworks and threat assessment protocols. This study presents a pedagogical framework for integrating generative AI (GenAI) into university-level cybersecurity curricula. The methodology establishes foundational knowledge in generative models and language processing architectures, followed by applications across defensive security measures. The framework includes automated cyber threat intelligence, malicious code and malware detection, log anomaly detection, digital image forensics, and AI-assisted penetration testing. The framework acknowledges the dual-use nature of GenAI in security domains, incorporating prompt injection attacks that manipulate model behaviors and compromise system integrity. Laboratory modules presented will provide students with hands-on experience on advanced tools including large language models, diffusion models, and cognitive architectures for automated security assessment. This study seeks to prepare cybersecurity professionals with critical competencies necessary for effective operation within an environment increasingly shaped by artificial intelligence systems. © 2025 IEEE.

[R0089] year=2026 | venue=Proceedings of ACM Symposium on Access Control Models and Technologies, SACMAT | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Towards Agentic AI for Access Control in Cyber-infrastructures: Exploring the Security and Human Factors: [BlueSky Paper]
KEYWORDS: agentic access control; intelligence collection; intelligence orchestration; policy enforcement; policy evaluation; policy specification; Access control; Artificial intelligence; Human computer interaction; Human engineering; Intelligent agents; Life cycle; Network security; Security systems; Specifications; Access control policies; Agentic access control; Cyberinfrastructure; Effectiveness and efficiencies; Emerging technologies; Intelligence collection; Intelligence orchestration; Policy enforcement; Policy evaluation; Policy specification; Efficiency
ABSTRACT: Access Control (AC) remains one of the fundamental paradigms of computer security due to its potential to mitigate serious threats by restricting access to sensitive resources within emerging technologies. However, despite decades of research, the life-cycle, i.e., specification, evaluation, and enforcement, of AC policies in modern cyber-infrastructures remains incomplete, inaccurate, and unverified, which largely decreases their effectiveness and efficiency to counteract emerging threats and vulnerabilities.The recent unparalleled advancements in the field of Generative Artificial Intelligence (AI) present a unique opportunity to address these challenges by introducing AC-Agents, an innovative solution for not only handling the life-cycle of AC policies, but also for collecting relevant threat intelligence and orchestrating the deployment of counteractions in a seamless, automated, and larger scale fashion with a high degree of effectiveness and efficiency.This paper presents an overview of existing problems affecting modern cyber-infrastructures, discusses potential solutions leveraging emerging AI agents, and elaborates on the security and human factors involved, hoping to inspire a thoughtful discussion within the AC, the security, and the AI communities, such that future approaches can benefit from interdisciplinary collaborations. © 2026 Owner/Author.

[R0090] year=2026 | venue=Proceedings of 2026 International Conference on Artificial Intelligence and Fintech, IC-AIF 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Teaching GPT to Explain Itself: An Interpretable AI Framework for Rejecting Loan Applications in Regulated Finance
KEYWORDS: Compliance Explanation; Explainable AI (XAI); GPT Large Model; LoRA Fine-Tuning; Regulated Finance; Artificial intelligence; Compliance control; Computation theory; Finance; Human engineering; Risk perception; Black boxes; Compliance explanation; Explainable AI (XAI); Fine tuning; GPT large model; Large models; LoRA fine-tuning; Regulated finance; Risk signals; Stringents; Regulatory compliance
