[R0101] year=2025 | venue=2025 8th International Conference on Circuit, Power and Computing Technologies, ICCPCT 2025 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Evaluating and Exploiting Security Vulnerabilities in Large Language Models (LLMs) for Offensive Penetration Testing
KEYWORDS: AI Red Teaming; LLM Security; Multi-turn Exploits; Offensive Penetration Testing; Prompt Injection; Token Economy Poisoning; Artificial intelligence; Information leakage; Security of data; Security systems; AI red teaming; Language model; Large language model security; Model security; Multi-turn; Multi-turn exploit; Offensive penetration testing; Penetration testing; Prompt injection; Red teaming; Token economy poisoning; Safety engineering
ABSTRACT: Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly vital in AI systems, but their security vulnerabilities require deeper investigation. This paper introduces a novel offensive penetration testing framework designed to systematically evaluate these security risks in leading LLMs. We probed six state-of-the-art models using five distinct exploit strategies, including prompt injection, multi-turn jailbreaks, and context manipulation techniques like Hybrid Agent Roleplay Exploitation (HARE) and Token Economy Poisoning. Our experiments demonstrate significant security gaps: many tested LLMs suffer from context-dependent security failures, delayed moderation responses, and the potential for stealthy leakage of sensitive information. By quantifying each model's vulnerability through success rate, stealth, and novelty metrics, we reveal varying degrees of susceptibility. Based on these findings, we propose targeted alignment strategies to address the identified weaknesses. This research underscores the urgent need for robust security measures and contributes towards building more secure, context-aware LLMs suitable for safety-critical applications. © 2025 IEEE.

[R0102] year=2026 | venue=IEEE Network | type=Article
